  "lesson2Card1Title": "What is a data type?",
  "@lesson2Card1Speech": {
    "description": "Lesson 2 text. Very simple English: short sentences, one idea each. int, float, str, bool, type and `backticks` are code: keep them in English."
  },
  "lesson2Card1Speech": "Every value has a kind. We call it a data type. Python needs to know the kind of each value. A number is one kind. A word is another kind.",
  "lesson2Card1Tip": "A data type tells Python what kind of value it is.",
  "lesson2Card2Title": "int: whole numbers",
  "lesson2Card2Speech": "An `int` is a whole number. It has no decimal point. My lives are a whole number. I can have 3 lives, not 2.5 lives!",
  "lesson2Card2Tip": "`int` is short for integer. It means whole number.",
  "lesson2Card3Title": "float: numbers with a dot",
  "lesson2Card3Speech": "A `float` is a number with a decimal point. My speed can be 2.5. That dot makes it a float.",
  "lesson2Card3Tip": "`10.0` is a float too. The dot is what counts.",
  "lesson2Card4Title": "str: text in quotes",
  "lesson2Card4Speech": "A `str` is text. Text always goes inside quotes. My name is text: `\"Cog\"`. Be careful! `\"5\"` in quotes is text, not a number.",
  "lesson2Card4Tip": "Quotes make it text. `\"5\"` is a str. `5` is an int.",
  "lesson2Card5Title": "bool: True or False",
  "lesson2Card5Speech": "A `bool` has only two values. It is `True` or `False`. It answers a yes-or-no question. Am I online? `True`! Always use a big T and a big F.",
  "lesson2Card5Tip": "`true` with a small t does not work. Use `True`.",
  "lesson2Card6Title": "type(): ask Python",
  "lesson2Card6Speech": "Not sure about a type? Just ask Python! Use `type()` to ask. Use `print()` to show the answer on the screen.",
  "lesson2Card6Tip": "`<class 'float'>` means the value is a float.",
  "lesson2Card7Title": "Recap: 4 data types",
  "lesson2Card7Speech": "Great job! You know 4 data types now. Let's look at them side by side.",
  "lesson2Card7Tip": "Numbers: `int` and `float`. Text: `str`. Yes or no: `bool`.",
