  "@lesson8Card1Speech": {
    "description": "Lesson 8 text. Very simple English: short sentences, one idea each. list, item, append, len, in, for, if, variable, string and `backticks` are code words: keep them in English."
  },
  "lesson8Card1Speech": "Think of your backpack in a game. It holds many items, in order: a sword, a shield, and a potion. A list is like that. In Lesson 1, a variable held one value. A list has one name, but many values!",
  "lesson8Card1Tip": "A list keeps many values in order, under one name.",
  "lesson8Card2Title": "Making a list",
  "lesson8Card2Speech": "Use square brackets `[ ]`. Put the items inside, with a comma between them. A long list can go on more lines, like this. Python shows strings in a list with `'` quotes. That is OK!",
  "lesson8Card2Tip": "`[` starts the list, commas go between items, and `]` ends it.",
  "lesson8Card3Title": "Warning: the first item is [0]",
  "lesson8Card3Speech": "Use `[ ]` to pick one item. Careful! The first item is `[0]`, not `[1]`. Just like `\"Cog\"[0]` in Lesson 7, and `range()` in Lesson 4. Counting starts at 0.",
  "lesson8Card3Tip": "3 items: `[0]`, `[1]`, `[2]`.",
  "lesson8Card4Title": "Warning: going too far",
  "lesson8Card4Speech": "The backpack has 3 items: `[0]`, `[1]`, and `[2]`. There is no `[3]`! If you ask for it, Python shows an error. The last item is `[2]`.",
  "lesson8Card4Tip": "With 3 items, the last one is `[2]`. It is always one less than the number of items.",
  "lesson8Card5Title": "Changing an item",
  "lesson8Card5Speech": "You found an axe! Put it in place `[0]`. Write `items[0] = \"axe\"`. The sword is gone, and the axe takes its place.",
  "lesson8Card5Tip": "Use `=` with `[ ]` to change one item, like changing a variable.",
  "lesson8Card6Title": "Counting items: len()",
  "lesson8Card6Speech": "In Lesson 7, `len()` counted the characters in a string. With a list, `len()` counts the items. The backpack has 3 items, so `len(items)` gives 3.",
  "lesson8Card6Tip": "List: `len()` counts items. String: `len()` counts characters.",
  "lesson8Card7Title": "Adding an item: append()",
  "lesson8Card7Speech": "You found a map! Use `.append()` to add it. The new item goes at the end of the list. Now the backpack has 4 items.",
  "lesson8Card7Tip": "`.append()` always adds at the end.",
  "lesson8Card8Title": "Looping through a list",
  "lesson8Card8Speech": "Remember the `for` loop from Lesson 4? It works with lists too! `for item in items:` picks one item at a time. First sword, then shield, then potion. No `range()` needed!",
  "lesson8Card8Tip": "The loop runs once for each item in the list.",
  "lesson8Card9Title": "Checking with in",
  "lesson8Card9Speech": "Is there a potion in the backpack? Ask with `in`. The answer is `True` or `False`. Use it in an `if`, like in Lesson 3. If you have a potion, you can heal!",
  "lesson8Card9Tip": "`in` asks: is this item in the list?",
  "lesson8Card10Title": "Recap: lists",
  "lesson8Card10Speech": "Great job! Here are all your list tools on one screen.",
