  "@lesson9Title": {
    "description": "Title of Lesson 9 on the lesson trail."
  },
  "lesson9Title": "Input and Output",
  "@lesson9TypedLabel": {
    "description": "Output box key under rows with a typed answer: the keyboard chip shows what the person typed (not what the program printed)."
  },
  "lesson9TypedLabel": "Typed by the person",
  "lesson9Card1Title": "Programs talk to people",
  "@lesson9Card1Speech": {
    "description": "Lesson 9 text. Very simple English: short sentences, one idea each. print, input, int, float, str, Enter, variable and `backticks` are code words: keep them in English."
  },
  "lesson9Card1Speech": "A game asks you: What is your name? You type your name. Then the game says hi! Output is what the program shows. We use `print()`. Input is what the person types. We use `input()`.",
  "lesson9Card1Tip": "Output: the program shows. Input: the person types.",
  "lesson9Card2Title": "print() with more things",
  "lesson9Card2Speech": "`print()` can show many things. Put commas between them. Python adds a space between each thing for you! With `+` from Lesson 7, you add the space yourself, and numbers need `str()`.",
  "lesson9Card2Tip": "Commas: Python adds the spaces. `+`: you add them.",
  "lesson9Card3Title": "Asking with input()",
  "lesson9Card3Speech": "`input()` shows a question. Then the program waits. It waits until the person types and presses Enter. What they typed goes into the variable. Here, Ana types a name.",
  "lesson9Card3Tip": "Put a space at the end of the question, so the answer does not touch it.",
  "lesson9Card4Title": "Using what they typed",
  "lesson9Card4Speech": "Now `name` holds what the person typed. Use it like any string! Join it with `+`, just like in Lesson 7.",
  "lesson9Card4Tip": "What the person types is a string. You can join it, print it, or check it.",
  "lesson9Card5Title": "Warning: input() always gives text",
  "lesson9Card5Speech": "Big warning! `input()` ALWAYS gives text, a `str`. Even when the person types 12, `age` is `\"12\"`, not 12. So `age + 1` is text + number. Remember `\"5\" + 5` from Lesson 7? Same error!",
  "lesson9Card5Tip": "Typed numbers come in as text. You must turn them into numbers.",
  "lesson9Card6Title": "int(): text to a whole number",
  "lesson9Card6Speech": "Wrap `input()` in `int()`. `int()` turns text into a whole number. Now `\"12\"` becomes 12, and `age + 1` gives 13. For decimal numbers, use `float()`, like the floats in Lesson 2.",
  "lesson9Card6Tip": "Need a number from the person? Use `int(input())`.",
  "lesson9Card7Title": "Warning: int() needs a real number",
  "lesson9Card7Speech": "What if the person types letters? `int(\"abc\")` gives an error. `int()` only works with real numbers, like `\"42\"`.",
