  "lesson10Title": "Logic",
  "lesson10Card1Title": "Combining questions",
  "@lesson10Card1Speech": {
    "description": "Lesson 10 text. Very simple English: short sentences, one idea each. and, or, not, True, False, if, else and `backticks` are code words: keep them in English."
  },
  "lesson10Card1Speech": "Mom says: You can play if you did your homework AND it is Saturday. That is two questions in one! In Lesson 3, each `if` asked only one question. Now we can ask two at once.",
  "lesson10Card1Tip": "Use `and`, `or`, and `not` to join questions.",
  "lesson10Card2Title": "and: both must be True",
  "lesson10Card2Speech": "`and` is `True` only when BOTH sides are `True`. If even one side is `False`, the answer is `False`. Look at the table: only the top row gives `True`.",
  "lesson10Card2Tip": "`and`: both must be `True`.",
  "lesson10Card3Title": "or: at least one True",
  "lesson10Card3Speech": "`or` is `True` when at least ONE side is `True`. It is `False` only when both sides are `False`. The door opens with a key or the secret code!",
  "lesson10Card3Tip": "`or`: one `True` is enough.",
  "lesson10Card4Title": "not: flip it",
  "lesson10Card4Speech": "`not` flips the answer. `not True` is `False`. `not False` is `True`. Here, the game is not over, so we keep playing!",
  "lesson10Card4Tip": "`not` turns `True` into `False`, and `False` into `True`.",
  "lesson10Card5Title": "Using and inside if",
  "lesson10Card5Speech": "Put `and` inside an `if`, like in Lesson 3. The door opens only if you have the key AND the battery is more than 0. Here, the battery is 0, so the `else` part runs.",
  "lesson10Card5Tip": "If the whole question is `False`, the `else` part runs.",
  "lesson10Card6Title": "Warning: and is not or",
  "lesson10Card6Speech": "Careful! `and` and `or` give different answers with the same values. A robot needs lives AND battery to move. This robot has 3 lives but no battery. With `or`, it says yes. That is a bug!",
  "lesson10Card6Tip": "Need both? Use `and`. One is enough? Use `or`.",
  "lesson10Card7Title": "Order: not, and, then or",
  "lesson10Card7Speech": "Python does `not` first, then `and`, then `or`. So in `a or b and c`, the `b and c` part goes first. Brackets change the order, just like in Lesson 6. Here, `a` is `True`, and `b` and `c` are `False`.",
  "lesson10Card7Tip": "Not sure what goes first? Add brackets.",
  "lesson10Card8Title": "Between two numbers",
  "lesson10Card8Speech": "Is the level from 1 to 3? You can ask with `and`, like `level >= 1 and level <= 3`. Python also lets you write it short: `1 <= level <= 3`. Both mean the same!",
  "lesson10Card8Tip": "`1 <= level <= 3` means: level is between 1 and 3.",
  "lesson10Card9Title": "Recap: logic",
  "lesson10Card9Speech": "Great job! Here are and, or, and not on one screen.",
  "lesson10Card9Tip": "`and`: both. `or`: at least one. `not`: flip it.",
